// Original summaries and questions based on the user-supplied 2001 ESCO manual.
// Historical legal thresholds are intentionally not presented as current rules.
export const epaNotice = 'Your preparation manual is the 2001 ESCO edition. These original lessons explain its technical concepts; dated fines, sales restrictions, phaseout schedules, leak-rate thresholds, recovery tables, and exam administration rules must be checked against current EPA requirements. This app provides practice only and cannot issue EPA certification.';
export const epaScopes = {
 universal:{name:'Universal · Core + Types I, II & III',categories:['epa-core','epa-type1','epa-type2','epa-type3'],count:100,minutes:120},
 type1:{name:'Type I · Core + small appliances',categories:['epa-core','epa-type1'],count:50,minutes:60},
 type2:{name:'Type II · Core + high-pressure appliances',categories:['epa-core','epa-type2'],count:50,minutes:60},
 type3:{name:'Type III · Core + low-pressure appliances',categories:['epa-core','epa-type3'],count:50,minutes:60}
};
const unit=(id,title,summary,pages,sections,takeaways,items)=>({id,track:'epa',title,weight:25,minutes:Math.ceil(sections.map(s=>s[1]).join(' ').split(/\s+/).length/140)+2,icon:id==='epa-core'?'shield':'system',summary,pages,sections:sections.map(([title,text])=>({title,text})),takeaways,references:['epa-manual','epa-certification','epa'],questions:items.map(([prompt,correct,a,b,c,explanation],i)=>({id:`${id}-${i+1}`,category:id,prompt,options:[correct,a,b,c],answer:0,explanation}))});
export const epaCategories = [
unit('epa-core','Core · Refrigerant Fundamentals','The environmental, recovery, and safety foundation shared by every certification type.','Introduction and printed pp. 1–5',[
 ['Certification roadmap & refrigeration cycle','The manual divides preparation into Core, Type I (small appliances), Type II (high- and very-high-pressure appliances, excluding small appliances and motor vehicle air conditioning), and Type III (low-pressure appliances). Universal combines the types. Core is the shared knowledge foundation, not a stand-alone service certification. Section 609 covers motor vehicle air conditioning separately. In the vapor-compression cycle, a compressor raises vapor pressure, a condenser rejects heat, a metering device reduces pressure, and an evaporator absorbs heat. Follow refrigerant flow rather than relying on where a component sits.'],
 ['Ozone depletion, ODP & the Montreal Protocol','CFCs and HCFCs contain chlorine that can participate in stratospheric ozone destruction. Ozone helps shield Earth from harmful ultraviolet radiation. Ozone depletion potential (ODP) compares effects on stratospheric ozone; it is not a measurement of global warming potential (GWP). HFCs lack chlorine and have zero ODP, but that does not mean zero climate impact or unrestricted handling. The Montreal Protocol established international controls on ozone-depleting substances. The manual’s future-looking phaseout language is historical; check current phaseout and refrigerant transition rules.'],
 ['Clean Air Act, records & the three Rs','Recover means removing refrigerant and storing it in an external container without necessarily processing it. Recycle means cleaning it for reuse, typically by oil separation and filtration. Reclaim means processing it to the applicable purity standard and verifying quality; current references use AHRI terminology. Do not intentionally vent regulated refrigerant during service. Minimize incidental releases with proper procedures and low-loss fittings. Maintain required records and use the appropriate certification and recovery equipment. The manual’s dollar penalties, equipment-registration wording, and refrigerant sales restrictions are dated and are not current compliance instructions.'],
 ['Devices, recovery techniques & contamination','A self-contained recovery device has its own means of removing refrigerant; a system-dependent device relies on appliance components or conditions. Verify device certification, refrigerant compatibility, operating limits, valve positions, condition, and manufacturer instructions before use. Never combine unknown or different refrigerants in a clean recovery cylinder; isolate and label contaminated mixtures for appropriate handling. Short, properly sized hoses reduce pressure drop and trapped refrigerant. Recovering liquid first can speed removal when the equipment and procedure permit it; remaining vapor still needs recovery. Prevent liquid trapping between closed valves.'],
 ['Substitutes, oils & blend behavior','A substitute refrigerant is not automatically a drop-in replacement. Check compressor, seals, metering device, oil, pressure rating, and retrofit instructions. Hygroscopic oils readily absorb moisture, so keep them sealed and minimize exposure. Temperature glide is the range of saturation temperatures during phase change for a blend. Fractionation changes mixture composition when components separate at different rates. Follow liquid-withdrawal and charging instructions for blends, with controlled metering where required to avoid liquid slugging. Use the correct dew/bubble data for the measurement. Do not apply the manual’s broad oil statements to every modern refrigerant.'],
 ['Dehydration, cylinders, safety & shipping','Recovery removes refrigerant; subsequent evacuation/dehydration removes air and moisture. A micron gauge measures deep absolute vacuum more usefully than a general manifold compound gauge. Isolate the pump and watch vacuum behavior; a rise can reflect moisture, refrigerant release, or a leak and requires diagnosis. Use approved refillable recovery cylinders with adequate pressure rating, inspection/requalification, and refrigerant identification. Determine the allowed fill by the cylinder and refrigerant specifications, tare weight, and a scale; the manual’s 80% concept addresses expansion space and is not a universal 80%-of-gross-weight formula. Never refill disposable cylinders. Use task-specific PPE, ventilation, SDS guidance, and regulated dry nitrogen within equipment limits; never oxygen for pressure testing. Never use a flame to heat cylinders or intentionally smell refrigerant. Follow current DOT packaging, marking, transport, and shipping-paper rules rather than relying on cylinder paint color.']
],['Core supports every certification type; Section 609 is separate.','ODP and GWP measure different environmental effects.','Recover, recycle, and reclaim are distinct processes.','Recovery and dehydration are separate steps.','Identify refrigerant, cylinder limits, and current requirements before work.'],[
 ['What does recovering refrigerant mean?','Removing it from an appliance into an external container','Restoring purity to a certified specification automatically','Venting it until pressure is zero','Replacing it with nitrogen','Recovery removes and stores refrigerant; it does not by itself clean it or verify reclaimed purity. Manual: Core, printed p. 2.'],
 ['What distinguishes reclamation from ordinary recovery?','Processing to the applicable purity specification with quality verification','Using a bigger storage tank only','Running the appliance compressor longer','Adding oil to a contaminated cylinder','Reclamation addresses refrigerant quality, while recovery can collect refrigerant in any condition. Manual: Core, p. 2.'],
 ['What is the purpose of refrigerant recycling?','Reduce contaminants through processes such as oil separation and filtration','Destroy refrigerant by combustion','Mix multiple refrigerants into a single product','Remove the need for any recovery equipment','Recycling cleans refrigerant for reuse; it is different from removal alone and from full reclamation. Manual: Core, p. 2.'],
 ['Which refrigerant families contain chlorine associated with ozone depletion?','CFCs and HCFCs','HFCs only','Carbon dioxide only','All refrigerants equally','CFC and HCFC chemistry includes chlorine. HFCs do not contain chlorine. Manual: Core, p. 1.'],
 ['What does zero ozone depletion potential establish?','No ozone-depleting effect under that metric, not zero climate impact','Zero global warming potential in every case','Permission to vent the refrigerant','Compatibility with every system','ODP and GWP are different measures. A zero-ODP refrigerant can still have climate and regulatory consequences. Manual: Core, p. 1.'],
 ['Why is stratospheric ozone important?','It reduces harmful ultraviolet radiation reaching Earth','It is the refrigerant inside every evaporator','It makes cylinder overfilling safe','It prevents every greenhouse effect','Stratospheric ozone helps protect against harmful ultraviolet exposure. Ozone depletion is the environmental focus of the manual’s Core section.'],
 ['What is the Montreal Protocol associated with?','International controls on ozone-depleting substances','A universal wiring color code','Recovery cylinder tare weights','Minimum duct velocity','The Montreal Protocol addresses ozone-depleting substances. Its historical schedules should not be mistaken for current transition guidance. Manual: Core, p. 2.'],
 ['What does a self-contained recovery device have?','Its own means of drawing refrigerant from the appliance','A requirement that the appliance compressor always run','No external storage requirement in any use','Permission to discharge refrigerant to the air','Self-contained recovery equipment supplies its own recovery mechanism instead of depending on the appliance compressor. Manual: Core, p. 2.'],
 ['What characterizes system-dependent recovery?','It relies on components or conditions of the appliance','It always restores refrigerant to virgin purity','It is suitable for every appliance size','It eliminates the need to identify refrigerant','System-dependent equipment relies on the appliance rather than an independent recovery mechanism. Its use has applicability limits. Manual: Core, p. 2.'],
 ['Why are low-loss hose fittings used?','To minimize refrigerant release during connection and disconnection','To permit intentional venting','To identify refrigerant solely by hose color','To replace cylinder pressure relief protection','Low-loss connections reduce incidental emissions. They do not remove the need for correct recovery procedures. Manual: introduction and Core.'],
 ['What should happen when an unidentified refrigerant mixture is recovered?','Keep it isolated and labeled for appropriate handling','Add it to a clean cylinder of known refrigerant','Identify it by smelling the gas','Sell it as virgin refrigerant without processing','Mixing contaminates otherwise usable recovered refrigerant and can complicate reclamation. Never use deliberate exposure to identify it. Manual: Core, p. 3.'],
 ['What does hygroscopic mean when describing refrigerant oil?','It readily absorbs moisture','It repels all water permanently','It is safe to mix with every oil','It cannot be contaminated','Hygroscopic lubricants absorb moisture from air. Keep containers sealed and follow product-specific oil requirements. Manual: Core, p. 3.'],
 ['What is temperature glide?','A blend’s range of saturation temperatures during phase change','The temperature setting on a thermostat','The maximum permitted cylinder surface temperature','A fixed pressure drop across every filter','Some blends evaporate or condense across a temperature range. This affects charging and interpretation of saturation data. Manual: Core, p. 3.'],
 ['Why is a refrigerant retrofit not automatically a drop-in substitution?','Oil, components, controls, and pressure compatibility must be evaluated','Every refrigerant has identical properties','Only the service label ever needs changing','The compressor will identify the refrigerant automatically','A retrofit must follow approved equipment and refrigerant procedures; a new refrigerant is not automatically compatible with the existing system. Manual: Core, p. 3.'],
 ['What is the main purpose of system dehydration?','Remove moisture and water vapor','Store refrigerant for transport','Add refrigerant oil','Increase the refrigerant charge','Dehydration removes moisture after appropriate recovery. Moisture can contribute to acid formation and system damage. Manual: Core, p. 4.'],
 ['Which instrument is most useful for measuring a deep evacuation?','A micron gauge','A tape measure','A thermostat alone','A standard ammeter alone','A micron gauge measures absolute pressure in the deep-vacuum range more accurately than a typical compound service gauge. Manual: Core, p. 4.'],
 ['Why isolate a system from the vacuum pump during a vacuum holding check?','To observe the system’s own pressure behavior','To prevent all remaining moisture from evaporating','To guarantee a leak cannot exist','To convert vacuum into refrigerant charge','A holding check helps evaluate pressure rebound without the running pump masking system behavior. Diagnose the cause of any rise. Manual: Core, p. 4.'],
 ['Which cylinder is appropriate for refrigerant recovery?','An approved refillable recovery cylinder rated for the refrigerant','Any empty disposable supply cylinder','An unmarked container that happens to seal','A cylinder selected by paint color alone','Use an approved, rated, inspected recovery cylinder with the correct fill limit and identification. A disposable cylinder must not be refilled. Manual: Core, pp. 4–5.'],
 ['Why must a recovery cylinder have expansion space?','Liquid refrigerant expands as temperature increases','Refrigerant cannot exert pressure','Expansion space guarantees no inspection is needed','An overfilled cylinder becomes lighter when heated','Overfilling can create dangerous pressure as liquid expands. Follow rated fill limits and use a scale; do not improvise from gross weight. Manual: Core, p. 4.'],
 ['What is the best way to control cylinder filling?','Use a scale and the applicable cylinder/refrigerant fill limit','Stop only when the cylinder feels heavy','Use paint color as a mass measurement','Ignore tare weight','Fill control requires mass information and the cylinder’s applicable specification. Tare weight must be distinguished from refrigerant weight. Manual: Core, p. 4.'],
 ['Which gas should never be used to pressure-test a refrigeration system?','Oxygen','Regulated dry nitrogen within rated limits','The test gas specified by a supported manufacturer procedure','A compatible inert test medium specified for the system','Oxygen can react dangerously with oil and other materials. Use approved procedures and regulated inert gas within limits. Manual: Core safety.'],
 ['What does a refrigerant safety data sheet help determine?','Exposure hazards, protective measures, and emergency response','The customer’s credit limit','The exact size of every duct','Whether an appliance is legally owned','SDS information supports hazard assessment, PPE, ventilation, storage, and response planning. Manual: Core safety and shipping.'],
 ['Which certification program addresses motor vehicle air conditioning separately from Section 608?','Section 609','Type III alone','Core alone','A contractor license by itself','Section 609 applies to motor vehicle air conditioning; Section 608 Types I–III address different stationary appliance groups. Manual: introduction.'],
 ['What should be done with the manual’s 2001 penalty amounts and future phaseout dates?','Treat them as historical and consult current EPA information','Assume they are unchanged today','Use them instead of current law','Hide their source date','The supplied edition is historical. Its legal amounts, deadlines, restrictions, and schedules are not verified current guidance.'],
 ['Does completing this app’s Universal practice exam grant EPA certification?','No; certification requires the applicable approved testing process','Yes, if overall accuracy exceeds 70%','Yes, if the timer is enabled','Yes, if a screenshot is saved','This app is independent practice material. It does not administer an approved certification examination or issue credentials.']
]),
unit('epa-type1','Type I · Small Appliances','Practice safe recovery from factory-sealed, small-charge appliances.','Printed pp. 6–7, with Core pp. 2–5',[
 ['Scope & equipment requirements','The manual describes small appliances as factory-manufactured, charged, and hermetically sealed appliances containing five pounds of refrigerant or less. Examples include qualifying household refrigerators, freezers, and window air conditioners. A small physical cabinet alone does not establish the legal classification. Split systems are not automatically small appliances because their charge is low. Type I or Universal preparation builds on Core; verify current EPA definitions and testing requirements.'],
 ['Recovery targets & compressor condition','The manual distinguishes recovery equipment by manufacture date and whether the appliance compressor operates. It describes the familiar 80%/90% recovery concepts and a four-inch-Hg alternative for qualifying procedures; the exact applicability must be checked against current rules and equipment instructions. Do not confuse a percentage of recovered charge with cylinder fill capacity. An operating appliance compressor can assist a system-dependent process. A nonoperating compressor may require access to both sides and measures to release refrigerant trapped in oil. Confirm results rather than assuming a single hose connection is enough.'],
 ['Access, trapped refrigerant & leak checks','Identify the refrigerant and appliance before connecting equipment. Leak-test newly installed access fittings. Temporary piercing devices can leak over time and should not automatically become permanent repairs. Refrigerant can remain dissolved in compressor oil or trapped in components even after gauge pressure appears low. Use the manufacturer’s controlled recovery procedures, appropriate access, and rated equipment. Avoid open-flame heating or improvised methods that could ignite refrigerant, oil, or nearby materials.'],
 ['Leak repair context & contamination','Do not transfer the manual’s historical large-appliance leak-rate rules to all small appliances. Diagnose actual leaks, protect recovered refrigerant from contamination, and check current disposal and service requirements. An appliance that uses an unfamiliar refrigerant—such as an old toxic refrigerant, a flammable refrigerant, or an absorption-system working fluid—requires compatible equipment and training. Ordinary fluorocarbon recovery equipment is not suitable for every substance. Compressor damage may contaminate refrigerant and oil; investigate safely without intentionally smelling the refrigerant.'],
 ['Safety, shipping & disposal','Apply Core cylinder, ventilation, electrical isolation, PPE, and shipping practices. Protect against cuts at access tubing and unexpected compressor operation. Do not vent refrigerant from a charging cylinder or service hose simply to adjust quantity. Verify required refrigerant removal before disposal, and document responsibilities in the disposal chain. Use current EPA and DOT instructions for removal verification, recovery performance, cylinder labeling, requalification, and transport.']
],['Small-appliance scope depends on construction and charge, not cabinet size.','Check compressor operation and recovery-equipment applicability.','Recover refrigerant trapped in oil and both sides as needed.','Unknown refrigerants require identification and compatible equipment.'],[
 ['Which characteristic is part of the manual’s small-appliance definition?','Factory charged and hermetically sealed with five pounds or less','Any air conditioner weighing less than 50 pounds','Every split system with a small indoor coil','Any appliance installed in a residence','The definition combines factory manufacture/charge, hermetic sealing, and a limited refrigerant charge; physical dimensions alone do not decide it. Manual: Type I, p. 6.'],
 ['Which is a typical small appliance when it meets the definition?','A factory-sealed household refrigerator','A large centrifugal chiller','A field-assembled commercial split system','A motor vehicle air conditioner','A qualifying refrigerator is a common Type I example. Chillers, split systems, and MVAC equipment have different classifications. Manual: Type I, p. 6.'],
 ['Does a field-assembled split system automatically become a Type I small appliance if its charge is low?','No; construction and the applicable definition also matter','Yes, based solely on charge','Yes, based solely on room size','Yes, whenever it has a thermostat','Small-appliance classification is not determined by charge quantity alone. Factory sealing and manufacture are part of the definition.'],
 ['What should be determined before using system-dependent recovery?','Whether the appliance compressor operates and the method is applicable','Only the color of the appliance','Only the owner’s preferred schedule','Only the length of the power cord','Compressor operation affects how refrigerant can be moved and whether the proposed recovery method can meet the required result. Manual: Type I, pp. 6–7.'],
 ['What can an operating appliance compressor do during an appropriate system-dependent recovery?','Help move refrigerant to the recovery connection','Automatically reclaim all refrigerant','Replace all cylinder fill controls','Eliminate the need to identify refrigerant','System-dependent recovery can use the appliance compressor to assist refrigerant movement, subject to the approved procedure. Manual: Type I, pp. 6–7.'],
 ['Why may both high- and low-side access be needed with a nonoperating compressor?','Refrigerant can remain isolated or trapped on either side','Both sides always contain different refrigerants','A second port eliminates every safety hazard','It makes the appliance a low-pressure chiller','A failed compressor cannot necessarily transfer refrigerant between sides. Access and recovery procedures must address trapped refrigerant. Manual: Type I, p. 7.'],
 ['Where can refrigerant remain after apparent initial recovery?','Dissolved in compressor oil','Only in the thermostat','Only in the electrical cord','Nowhere once a hose is disconnected','Refrigerant can remain in oil and later vaporize. Evaluate recovery completion with the proper method rather than a quick pressure observation alone. Manual: Type I, p. 7.'],
 ['What should be done after installing an access fitting?','Leak-test it before recovery','Assume every new fitting is leak-free','Remove all cylinder markings','Open the system to the room','A leaking access connection can release refrigerant or admit air and compromise recovery. Verify the fitting first. Manual: Type I, p. 7.'],
 ['Why should a temporary piercing valve not automatically remain as a permanent repair?','It may develop a leak over time','It permanently upgrades the appliance certification','It guarantees the refrigerant is pure','It makes electrical isolation unnecessary','Temporary access fittings can leak and should be handled according to the proper repair and manufacturer procedure. Manual: Type I, p. 7.'],
 ['An unfamiliar refrigerant is found in an old appliance. What is the next step?','Identify it and verify safe, compatible handling equipment','Connect any fluorocarbon recovery unit immediately','Mix it into a known R-134a cylinder','Identify it by direct inhalation','Older or unusual appliances may contain toxic, flammable, or incompatible substances. Identification and equipment compatibility come before recovery. Manual: Type I, p. 7.'],
 ['Why is a camping absorption refrigerator not automatically compatible with standard fluorocarbon recovery equipment?','Its working fluids and hazards may be different','All refrigerators use the same refrigerant','Only its paint differs','Absorption units contain no substances','Absorption equipment can use different working fluids. Use the equipment and handling procedure appropriate to the identified system. Manual: Type I, p. 7.'],
 ['What should guide recovery-equipment selection for a flammable refrigerant?','Equipment approval and instructions for that refrigerant and task','A similar hose thread alone','The cheapest available recovery unit','Only the cylinder’s external color','Refrigerant compatibility includes flammability and pressure ratings; physical connection compatibility is not enough. Apply Core safety principles and current product instructions.'],
 ['Should different refrigerants be combined in one recovery cylinder to save space?','No; keep them separated and identified','Yes, if both appliances are small','Yes, if both refrigerants have zero ODP','Yes, if the cylinder is gray','Combining refrigerants creates contamination and may prevent ordinary reclamation. Separate and identify recovered refrigerants. Manual: Core and Type I.'],
 ['What does an appliance pressure reading of 0 psig alone prove?','It does not prove all refrigerant has been removed','The appliance contains no refrigerant in any form','The compressor oil is refrigerant-free','The appliance may always be cut open immediately','Atmospheric gauge pressure does not establish complete removal; refrigerant may remain in oil or trapped locations. Follow the applicable recovery procedure.'],
 ['What is the safest approach to suspected contaminated compressor oil?','Use appropriate contamination tests and handling procedures','Smell escaping refrigerant deliberately','Pour it into a clean refrigerant cylinder','Ignore the equipment history','Contamination should be evaluated using suitable methods and PPE, not intentional inhalation or mixing with clean recovered refrigerant. Manual: Type I and Core.'],
 ['Which precaution applies during small-appliance recovery?','Monitor cylinder mass and rated fill limits','Fill the cylinder until liquid exits','Use a disposable cylinder for recovery','Disable all pressure protection','Small charges do not eliminate cylinder hazards. Use a suitable refillable cylinder and the proper fill control. Manual: Type I safety references Core.'],
 ['Does a small refrigerant charge justify intentional venting during service?','No; use the applicable recovery and handling procedure','Yes, because small appliances are unregulated','Yes, if a window is open','Yes, if the customer agrees','Small-appliance service still requires proper refrigerant handling. A small charge is not general permission to vent. Manual: Core and Type I.'],
 ['What should be verified before scrapping a regulated small appliance?','Required refrigerant removal and applicable verification responsibilities','Only that the door is closed','Only that the appliance has no power cord','Only that the thermostat is off','Disposal requires appropriate refrigerant removal and verification under the applicable rules; electrical disconnection alone does not remove refrigerant. Manual: Core and Type I.'],
 ['How do recovery efficiency and cylinder filling limits differ?','One concerns refrigerant removed from the appliance; the other concerns safe cylinder loading','They are always the same percentage of the same mass','Both are measures of voltage','Neither relates to refrigerant','The manual discusses percentages in different contexts. Do not confuse recovered-charge targets with cylinder expansion space and safe fill limits.'],
 ['Why must recovery hoses and fittings be checked for leaks?','Leaks can release refrigerant and admit contaminants','Leaks always improve recovery speed','Leaks automatically purify refrigerant','Small hoses cannot leak','Sound connections help achieve recovery performance, protect refrigerant quality, and minimize emissions. Manual: Core and Type I.'],
 ['Can one assume every factory-sealed appliance contains R-134a?','No; identify refrigerant from reliable equipment information','Yes, regardless of age','Yes, if it has a freezer','Yes, if it plugs into a wall','Small appliances use different refrigerants over time and by design. Verify refrigerant identity and equipment compatibility before service.'],
 ['Why must electric power be controlled during recovery access and repair?','Unexpected operation and exposed electrical parts can cause injury','A thermostat setting always isolates line voltage','Only large appliances have electrical hazards','Recovery cylinders disconnect the electricity','Electrical isolation and safe work procedures remain necessary on small appliances, including against unexpected compressor startup.'],
 ['What should be done with vapor displaced from a regulated refrigerant charging cylinder?','Handle and recover it through the applicable closed procedure','Vent it to adjust the charge','Discharge it into a water drain','Use it to smell-test the refrigerant','Do not intentionally vent regulated refrigerant during transfer. Use an appropriate recovery or closed-transfer procedure. Manual: Type I, p. 7.'],
 ['What source should determine the exact recovery target for today’s task?','Current applicable requirements and the approved equipment procedure','A guess based only on appliance color','A 2001 statement applied without checking context','The size of the technician’s truck','Recovery requirements depend on appliance and equipment details. The historical manual provides study context, not a substitute for current applicability checks.'],
 ['For Type I preparation, why also study Core?','Core covers shared environmental, recovery, cylinder, and safety knowledge','Core replaces every Type I appliance topic','Core alone certifies every equipment type','Core covers only duct construction','Core is the common foundation, while Type I adds small-appliance-specific scope and recovery techniques. Manual: introduction and Type I.']
]),
unit('epa-type2','Type II · High-Pressure Appliances','Leak detection, recovery, and safe service on high- and very-high-pressure systems.','Printed pp. 8–10, with Core pp. 2–5',[
 ['Scope & leak detection','Type II addresses high- and very-high-pressure appliances except small appliances and motor vehicle air conditioning. Verify the refrigerant and system category, not just whether the unit is used for comfort cooling. Inspect for oil traces and other evidence, use a suitable electronic or ultrasonic detector to locate a suspected area, and use an appropriate bubble solution to pinpoint an accessible leak. Nitrogen tests require a regulator and must stay within all equipment limits; never use oxygen or overpressurize. A low charge alone does not identify the leak location.'],
 ['Leak repair rules & historical thresholds','The 2001 manual lists 15% comfort-cooling and 35% commercial/industrial annual leak rates for certain large charges. These are historical figures, not current universal thresholds. Current applicability can depend on refrigerant, full charge, appliance use, and later rules. Check the current EPA Section 608 and HFC management requirements for thresholds, calculation, repair, verification, inspections, and records. For the underlying concept, an annualized leak rate relates refrigerant added over a measured interval to the full charge, using the applicable calculation method; it is not just the pounds added.'],
 ['Recovery preparation & techniques','Check recovery-machine condition, filters, oil where applicable, valves, refrigerant compatibility, and cylinder capacity. Prevent cross-contamination when switching refrigerants by following the manufacturer’s clearing procedure. Recover liquid first when the setup permits, then remove vapor. A cold environment and pressure losses in long hoses can slow recovery. Identify receivers, liquid traps, isolation valves, and parallel compressor equalization paths so the intended recovery boundary is actually isolated. Low-loss fittings and short suitable hoses reduce avoidable emissions.'],
 ['Recovery requirements & completion','Recovery targets depend on the appliance, refrigerant, charge, and applicable equipment-era rules. The manual’s printed p. 9 table is historical and must not be applied blindly to modern equipment. Understand the difference between psig, inches of Hg vacuum relative to atmosphere, and absolute pressure. Reach the applicable target, isolate and monitor pressure, and continue recovery if trapped refrigerant causes rebound. A routine evacuation vacuum pump is not an unrestricted substitute for a recovery device. A major repair in the manual includes removal of a compressor, condenser, evaporator, or auxiliary heat exchanger coil; verify the current regulatory definition for the actual work.'],
 ['Refrigeration notes & safety','Noncondensable air can raise condensing pressure. Moisture, acids, and debris can damage the system, so use appropriate contamination tests, driers, and dehydration procedures. Crankcase heaters help reduce off-cycle refrigerant migration into compressor oil; they do not repair leaks. Do not energize a hermetic compressor in a deep vacuum or run a reciprocating compressor against a closed discharge service valve. Maintain pressure relief protection and required machine-room monitoring/ventilation. Modern refrigerants may introduce flammability or other hazards beyond this older manual. Follow manufacturer procedures for charging, pressure limits, and safe service.']
],['Identify refrigerant and appliance classification first.','Historical 15%/35% leak figures are not current universal rules.','Isolate recovery boundaries and evaluate pressure rebound.','Protect against pressure, contamination, electrical, and refrigerant-specific hazards.'],[
 ['Which appliance group is addressed by Type II preparation?','High- and very-high-pressure appliances except small appliances and MVAC','Only low-pressure centrifugal chillers','Only household refrigerators','Only motor vehicle air conditioners','Type II covers the stated pressure appliance groups with the small-appliance and MVAC exclusions. Manual: Type II, p. 8.'],
 ['During a visual leak inspection, what can oil traces suggest?','A possible refrigerant leak to investigate','Certain proof that the thermostat failed','Proof the refrigerant is pure','Permission to add refrigerant without diagnosis','Oil can travel with leaking refrigerant and leave evidence. Locate and confirm the leak rather than assuming an oil trace proves every detail. Manual: Type II, p. 8.'],
 ['Which method can help pinpoint a suspected accessible leak?','A suitable bubble solution at the suspected location','Measuring the room’s light level','Guessing from equipment age alone','Changing the thermostat battery only','After locating a general area, a compatible bubble solution can reveal an accessible leaking joint. Manual: Type II, p. 8.'],
 ['What should control nitrogen test pressure?','The approved procedure and the lowest applicable component pressure limit','The nitrogen cylinder’s maximum pressure','A desire to hear a louder leak','The truck’s air-tire pressure','Use a regulator and stay within equipment and component limits. Cylinder pressure is not an acceptable test-pressure target. Manual: Type II and Core safety.'],
 ['Why must the manual’s 15%/35% leak-rate figures be treated cautiously?','They are historical and are not current universal thresholds','They apply to every refrigerant and charge forever','They are pressure measurements in psig','They replace all leak verification','The 2001 text predates later refrigerant rules. Verify current applicability, thresholds, repair duties, and records for the actual appliance.'],
 ['What information is fundamental to a leak-rate calculation?','Refrigerant added, full charge, and the applicable measurement interval/method','Only the equipment cabinet dimensions','Only the ambient humidity','Only the compressor voltage','Leak rate relates refrigerant loss to the full charge over a defined interval using the required calculation method; pounds added alone are insufficient.'],
 ['Why inspect recovery-machine filters and condition?','Contamination and restrictions can impair recovery and damage equipment','A filter determines the customer’s certification type','Filters remove the need for cylinder mass checks','A dirty filter guarantees better separation','Recovery equipment must be maintained and used within manufacturer instructions. Contaminants and restrictions affect performance. Manual: Type II, p. 8.'],
 ['Why clear recovery equipment properly before switching refrigerants?','To avoid contaminating the next recovered refrigerant','To make all refrigerants chemically identical','To eliminate pressure ratings','To replace certification requirements','Residual refrigerant can contaminate a later batch. Follow the device-specific clearing and service procedure. Manual: Type II, p. 8.'],
 ['What often reduces initial recovery time when equipment and procedure permit?','Remove accessible liquid before remaining vapor','Use the longest possible narrow hose','Close every access valve before starting','Vent vapor into the room first','Liquid recovery can move significant refrigerant mass efficiently, but remaining vapor and trapped refrigerant still require recovery. Manual: Type II, p. 8.'],
 ['Why can long, restrictive hoses slow recovery?','They add pressure drop between the appliance and recovery device','They automatically increase refrigerant purity','They convert vapor to nitrogen','They remove every pressure difference','Hose restriction reduces effective flow and can lengthen recovery. Use suitable short, properly sized connections. Manual: Core and Type II.'],
 ['What should be checked on a parallel compressor system before recovery from an isolated portion?','Valve boundaries and any equalization connections','Only the number of room thermostats','Only the cabinet color','Only the building’s postal address','An unisolated equalization path can defeat the intended recovery boundary. Confirm that the selected section is actually isolated. Manual: Type II, p. 9.'],
 ['What can pressure rebound after an initial recovery indicate?','Refrigerant remains trapped or dissolved and is vaporizing','The cylinder is automatically empty','A perfect vacuum was proved permanently','The system is ready to vent','Refrigerant can emerge from oil or trapped locations and raise pressure. Reevaluate and continue recovery as required. Manual: Type II, p. 9.'],
 ['Why is a dehydration vacuum pump not automatically interchangeable with a recovery machine?','They have different functions and handling requirements','Every vacuum pump reclaims refrigerant','Both devices always discharge to an approved cylinder','Recovery devices only measure temperature','Recovery captures refrigerant; dehydration removes air and moisture after recovery. A standard evacuation pump is not a general-purpose refrigerant recovery device.'],
 ['Which is an example of a major repair in the manual’s definition?','Removing the compressor','Replacing a thermostat battery only','Cleaning an exterior cabinet','Changing a room air filter only','The manual lists removal of the compressor, condenser, evaporator, or auxiliary heat exchanger coil as major repairs. Verify current applicability. Manual: Type II, p. 9.'],
 ['What does psig measure?','Pressure relative to atmospheric pressure','Absolute pressure starting at a perfect vacuum','Only water temperature','Refrigerant purity','Gauge pressure is referenced to the surrounding atmosphere; it is different from an absolute pressure measurement. Recovery units must be interpreted correctly.'],
 ['What can noncondensable air cause in a refrigeration system?','Elevated condensing/discharge pressure','Automatic improvement in efficiency','A guarantee of correct refrigerant charge','Elimination of all moisture','Air does not condense as the refrigerant does under normal condenser conditions and can elevate pressure and impair heat transfer. Manual: Type II, p. 10.'],
 ['What is a crankcase heater intended to help reduce?','Off-cycle refrigerant migration into compressor oil','All duct leakage','The need for a pressure relief device','Every mechanical bearing failure','Keeping the crankcase appropriately warm helps discourage refrigerant accumulation in oil during off periods. Manual: Type II, p. 9.'],
 ['Why should a hermetic compressor not be energized under deep vacuum?','Motor insulation and cooling conditions may be unsafe','Deep vacuum guarantees optimal motor cooling','It makes refrigerant identification unnecessary','The thermostat will always prevent current flow','The manual warns that a hermetic motor can be damaged under deep vacuum. Follow the manufacturer’s safe operating requirements. Manual: Type II, p. 9.'],
 ['What is hazardous about running a reciprocating compressor with its discharge service valve closed?','Dangerous pressure can develop','It is the standard way to prove full capacity','It prevents all motor damage','It automatically performs a safe recovery','Blocking discharge can create a serious pressure hazard. Never use this as an improvised operating or recovery method. Manual: Type II safety, p. 10.'],
 ['What is one purpose of a filter-drier?','Remove moisture and contaminants within its designed capacity','Replace all leak repair','Identify refrigerant by color','Increase system air leakage','Driers help control moisture and contaminants, but their capacity is finite and they do not substitute for proper evacuation and repair. Manual: Type II, p. 9.'],
 ['A system is low on refrigerant. What is the best inference?','Investigate the cause and locate any leak rather than assuming its location','The compressor must be replaced immediately','Every service joint is leak-free','The system should be topped off indefinitely without evaluation','A low charge is a symptom that needs diagnosis. It does not establish the source of loss or remove applicable leak-repair obligations.'],
 ['Which sources should determine the recovery target for a current high-pressure appliance?','Applicable current EPA requirements and supported equipment instructions','Only an old table without checking refrigerant or charge','Only the technician’s preferred gauge reading','Only the cylinder’s paint color','Targets are context-dependent. The manual’s historical table is not a universal current rule for all equipment and refrigerants.'],
 ['What should happen to contaminated recovered refrigerant?','Keep it identified and separate for appropriate handling','Blend it into a clean supply cylinder','Label it as virgin without testing','Vent it because contamination removes restrictions','Contamination does not permit venting or mislabeling. Separate material and use the appropriate reclamation or disposal route. Manual: Core and Type II.'],
 ['What must be considered when handling a newer flammable refrigerant in a Type II appliance?','Refrigerant-specific equipment approvals, ignition control, and ventilation','Only that the old manual calls some refrigerants A1','Assume every modern refrigerant is nonflammable','Use any recovery machine with a matching hose','Modern refrigerants can introduce hazards not covered by the 2001 edition. Verify the actual refrigerant’s classification and supported handling procedures.'],
 ['Before opening an appliance for a major repair, what must be established?','Completion of the applicable refrigerant recovery procedure','Only that the thermostat is off','Only that the invoice is paid','Only that the pressure gauge is attached','Correct recovery and safe isolation precede opening the circuit. Verify the applicable target and trapped-refrigerant conditions. Manual: Type II and Core.']
]),
unit('epa-type3','Type III · Low-Pressure Appliances','Understand air infiltration, chiller recovery, and protection against water freezing.','Printed pp. 11–12, with Core safety and dehydration',[
 ['Scope, leak behavior & detection','Type III concerns low-pressure appliances, including relevant chillers that can operate below atmospheric pressure. Leaks in a subatmospheric section tend to admit air and moisture rather than only discharge refrigerant. Frequent purge operation, excess moisture, or high condensing pressure can indicate infiltration. Inspect seals, fittings, tube bundles, and open-drive shaft seals as appropriate. Use approved controlled pressurization and leak-detection procedures; do not extrapolate the manual’s example pressures to every chiller. Stay within the manufacturer’s limits and relief design.'],
 ['Leak repair & purge systems','The manual repeats historical leak-repair thresholds that must be checked against later EPA rules. A purge unit removes noncondensable gases while attempting to limit refrigerant loss; it does not repair an air leak. Frequent purging is a symptom to investigate, not a justification for accepting chronic leakage. A water-box or tube-leak investigation requires the appropriate isolation, draining, test equipment, and manufacturer procedure. Refrigerant-side pressure and water-side condition must be considered together.'],
 ['Recovery techniques & water protection','For applicable chillers, remove liquid refrigerant first, then recover the considerable vapor that can remain. A liquid-empty vessel is not necessarily refrigerant-free. Refrigerant evaporation lowers temperature and can freeze water in tubes. Where the normal approved procedure calls for it, maintain the specified water circulation and recovery condenser cooling. If tube leaks are suspected, a different isolation/draining procedure may be necessary to prevent water entry; follow the chiller and recovery-machine instructions. Never generalize “always circulate” to a damaged tube bundle.'],
 ['Recovery requirements & pressure units','The manual distinguishes inches of Hg vacuum from mm Hg absolute and lists equipment-era evacuation values. These units have different reference points; 25 mm Hg absolute is not 25 inches Hg vacuum. Evaluate the applicable current recovery target, hold and monitor pressure, and repeat recovery if trapped refrigerant causes rebound. Distinguish recovery targets from deep-vacuum dehydration targets and from the chiller’s operating pressure. All handling must respect relief devices, vessel limits, and refrigerant-specific requirements.'],
 ['Recharging & refrigeration notes','Introducing liquid into a deep vacuum can produce rapid flashing and cooling that freezes water. The manual describes initial vapor charging for its example refrigerants; actual transition conditions must come from the chiller manufacturer and refrigerant data. Do not use one old R-11 example as a universal charging setpoint. Moisture and noncondensables impair operation; a vacuum holding test is evidence to interpret, not a perfect guarantee of dryness or tightness. Warming procedures, oil handling, and charge entry points must follow supported instructions.'],
 ['Safety, monitoring & relief protection','Low-pressure equipment still has serious pressure, electrical, chemical, and asphyxiation hazards. Never bypass a rupture disc or pressure-relief path to stop a leak. The manual’s nominal 15-psig rupture-disc example is equipment-specific historical context, not a replacement rating. Provide the refrigerant detection, ventilation, alarm, and emergency arrangements required for the actual machine room and refrigerant. Consult current safety classifications, SDS information, applicable standards, and manufacturer documentation rather than assuming every refrigerant shares the hazards of R-11 or R-123.']
],['Subatmospheric leaks can draw air and moisture into the chiller.','A purge unit treats noncondensables, not the leak itself.','Liquid recovery does not remove all remaining vapor.','Avoid freezing water during recovery and charging.','Absolute pressure and vacuum relative to atmosphere are different.'],[
 ['What commonly enters a chiller through a leak in a section below atmospheric pressure?','Air and moisture','Only additional pure refrigerant','Only compressor oil from outside','Nothing because a vacuum seals leaks','A subatmospheric pressure difference draws outside air and moisture inward through leaks. Manual: Type III, p. 11.'],
 ['What can frequent purge-unit operation suggest?','Ongoing air infiltration that needs investigation','Proof the chiller is leak-free','Automatic confirmation of correct charge','A need to remove all relief devices','Frequent purging can indicate a leak admitting air. The purge unit removes noncondensables but does not correct the opening. Manual: Type III, p. 12.'],
 ['What is a purge unit’s primary function?','Remove noncondensable gases while limiting refrigerant loss','Repair leaking tubes automatically','Replace the evaporator','Increase the amount of air in the condenser','Purge equipment deals with air and other noncondensables that enter a low-pressure system; it is not a leak repair. Manual: Type III, p. 12.'],
 ['Does a purge unit eliminate the need to repair an air leak?','No; the cause of infiltration still needs correction','Yes, whenever the chiller is running','Yes, if the purge runs continuously','Yes, if the leak is invisible','Continuous treatment of noncondensables does not correct the underlying leak and can cause refrigerant loss. Manual: Type III, p. 12.'],
 ['What is the usual recovery sequence described for applicable low-pressure chillers?','Liquid removal followed by vapor recovery','Vapor venting followed by opening the shell','Oil removal only','Recovery from the thermostat','Removing liquid first does not remove all refrigerant; substantial vapor can remain and must also be recovered. Manual: Type III, p. 11.'],
 ['After all visible liquid is removed, what may remain in a chiller?','A significant refrigerant vapor mass','Only clean atmospheric air','No refrigerant in any form','Only water with no vapor','A large vessel can contain considerable refrigerant vapor even after the liquid has been removed. Manual: Type III, p. 11.'],
 ['Why is water freezing a concern during refrigerant recovery?','Evaporation removes heat and can cool tubes below freezing','Recovery always adds unlimited heat','Water cannot freeze inside a chiller','Only electrical voltage affects freezing','Refrigerant evaporation can cool water enough to freeze and damage tubes; follow the proper water-side protection procedure. Manual: Type III, p. 11.'],
 ['When a tube leak is suspected, what should guide water-side preparation for recovery?','The chiller’s supported isolation/draining and recovery procedure','Always circulate water regardless of damage','Ignore water because recovery concerns only gas','Raise pressure without checking limits','A suspected tube leak changes the risk of water entry. Use the manufacturer’s procedure rather than a blanket circulation rule. Manual: Type III, p. 11.'],
 ['What should be checked before pressurizing a low-pressure chiller for leak detection?','Manufacturer limits, relief devices, and the approved test method','Only the service truck’s air supply','Only the thermostat setting','Only the owner’s preferred pressure','A low-pressure vessel can be damaged by excessive pressure. The manual’s examples are not universal ratings. Manual: Type III, p. 11.'],
 ['Are 25 mm Hg absolute and 25 inches Hg vacuum equivalent measurements?','No; the units and reference points differ','Yes, the numbers alone make them identical','Yes, on every manifold','Only if both are written in blue','Absolute pressure starts at perfect vacuum; inches Hg vacuum is typically relative to atmosphere. Unit and reference errors can be serious. Manual: Type III, p. 12.'],
 ['What does an absolute pressure scale reference?','A perfect vacuum','The current room temperature','Only local atmospheric pressure as zero','The cylinder’s gross weight','Absolute pressure is measured from zero pressure, unlike gauge pressure referenced to local atmosphere. Manual: Type III recovery units.'],
 ['Why monitor pressure after reaching a recovery target?','To detect rebound from remaining refrigerant or other conditions','To make a disconnected gauge accurate forever','To establish that all repairs are complete','To replace all leak testing','Pressure can rise as trapped or dissolved refrigerant vaporizes. Evaluate and continue recovery as required. Manual: Type III, p. 12.'],
 ['Why can introducing liquid refrigerant into a deep vacuum damage water-containing equipment?','Rapid flashing can lower temperature enough to freeze water','Liquid instantly becomes a perfect insulator','A vacuum prevents refrigerant boiling','Water becomes immune to freezing','Flashing refrigerant absorbs heat. The manufacturer’s charging procedure controls this freezing risk. Manual: Type III recharging, p. 11.'],
 ['What role can initial vapor charging serve in the manual’s chiller examples?','Raise pressure before liquid charging to reduce freezing risk','Remove all need for charge weighing','Replace the purge unit','Allow venting remaining vapor','The example procedure establishes suitable conditions before liquid charging. Use the actual equipment’s instructions rather than a historical universal setpoint. Manual: Type III, p. 11.'],
 ['Can the manual’s R-11 charging-pressure example be used for every chiller refrigerant?','No; refrigerant properties and manufacturer procedures differ','Yes, because all refrigerants boil identically','Yes, if the shell has the same color','Yes, if the technician knows the model year','Charging transition conditions are refrigerant- and equipment-specific. A historical R-11 example is not a universal instruction.'],
 ['What can air infiltration do to condensing pressure?','Raise it by adding noncondensables','Always lower it to a perfect vacuum','Guarantee exact design efficiency','Remove all refrigerant vapor','Noncondensables accumulate and can increase condenser pressure and reduce performance. Investigate infiltration rather than merely purging indefinitely. Manual: Type III, p. 12.'],
 ['What can excessive moisture collected at a purge unit indicate?','Possible water-side leakage requiring investigation','Certain proof of a dry system','A perfect refrigerant charge','A reason to bypass every alarm','Moisture is evidence to investigate, including the possibility of tube leakage. Confirm the source with appropriate tests. Manual: Type III, p. 12.'],
 ['Where can an open-drive chiller develop a leak?','At the compressor shaft seal','Only at the room thermostat','Only at the building’s roof drain','Nowhere if it is a low-pressure system','Shaft seals are one potential leakage location in open-drive equipment. Inspect and test using the appropriate method. Manual: Type III, p. 11.'],
 ['What is the function of a rupture disc?','Provide designed overpressure relief','Measure refrigerant purity','Control room humidity','Replace the compressor’s electrical disconnect','A rupture disc is part of vessel pressure protection. Its specified rating and discharge arrangement must be maintained. Manual: Type III, p. 12.'],
 ['What should be done with a leaking or suspect pressure-relief device?','Use qualified service and the proper replacement/procedure','Cap it permanently so refrigerant cannot escape','Disable all pressure monitoring','Increase the vessel test pressure until it seals','Do not defeat overpressure protection. Isolate hazards and service the relief system according to the supported requirements. Manual: Type III safety.'],
 ['What should determine a machine room’s refrigerant monitoring and ventilation provisions?','Applicable current standards, refrigerant hazards, and equipment requirements','Only the 2001 manual’s examples','Only whether a window exists','Only the room’s paint color','Monitoring, alarms, ventilation, and emergency arrangements depend on the actual installation and applicable requirements. Historical examples are not a complete design specification.'],
 ['Does “low-pressure appliance” mean there are no pressure hazards?','No; vessels, recovery equipment, and relief systems still require protection','Yes, all pressure controls can be removed','Yes, any cylinder can be used','Yes, pressure testing has no limits','Low-pressure classification does not remove hazards from pressurization, recovery, charging, or blocked relief paths. Manual: Type III safety.'],
 ['Why should a historical recovery table be checked before use today?','Applicability can depend on current rules, appliance, refrigerant, and equipment','Only typography changes over time','Every refrigerant has the same target forever','Historical tables override all manufacturer pressure limits','Determine the current applicable target and supported procedure; do not universalize a table from a 2001 edition.'],
 ['How do refrigerant recovery and subsequent deep-vacuum dehydration differ?','Recovery captures refrigerant; dehydration removes air and moisture','Both are simply venting','Dehydration stores refrigerant in a cylinder automatically','Recovery is only an electrical test','These are distinct service stages with different goals and pressure criteria. Complete applicable recovery before dehydration. Manual: Core and Type III.'],
 ['Does an excellent overall Universal practice score compensate for a weak Core section in the app’s section-based study target?','No; each included section is assessed separately','Yes, only the overall average matters','Yes, if Type III is perfect','Yes, if the timer expires','The app reports a separate 18/25 study target for each included section, reflecting the manual’s section-based preparation approach. This is not an official certification result.']
])
];
